<?xml version="1.0"?>

<rotor name="mi17_tail_rotor">

    <diameter unit="FT">                  10.5 </diameter>
    <numblades>                             3 </numblades>
    <gearratio>                        4.2 </gearratio>
    <nominalrpm>                       1134.0 </nominalrpm>
    <chord unit="FT">                     0.8 </chord>
    <flappingmoment unit="SLUG*FT2">     2.5 </flappingmoment>
    <liftcurveslope>                      6.0 </liftcurveslope>
    <twist>                               0.0 </twist>
    <massmoment>                         0.8 </massmoment>
    <tiplossfactor>                       1.0 </tiplossfactor>
    <inflowlag>                          0.10 </inflowlag> 
    <hingeoffset unit="FT">             0.02 </hingeoffset>

    <!-- Tail rotor RPM is slaved to the main rotor via ExternalRPM=0,
         exactly matching the original (pre-gearbox) Mi-17 model's design
         and FGRotor.h's own documented tail-rotor pattern: "the rotor
         needs to be attached to a dummy engine" (mi17_tail_engine, a flat
         50 HP electric_engine, provides collective/control mapping and
         torque bookkeeping only) "and ExternalRPM 0 links the tail
         rotor's RPM to the main (=first, =0) rotor".

         thruster_engine_num for the main gearbox's shared rotor is the
         LOWEST referenced <input engine="x"> index (FGGearbox.cpp), which
         is 0 here (mi17_main_engine_1); deliberately "the same slot a
         single dummy engine would occupy for a non-gearbox rotor" per
         that file's own comment, so this ExternalRPM=0 reference resolves
         to the right property (propulsion/engine[0]/rotor-rpm) without
         needing to know anything about the gearbox internally.

         This only works because of a fix in src/models/propulsion/
         FGRotor.cpp: engines are always fully loaded before any <gearbox>
         is parsed (FGPropulsion::Load()'s two-pass design), so when this
         tail rotor constructs, engine 0's thruster (owned by the gearbox)
         does not exist yet. FGRotor's ExternalRPM binding used to resolve
         eagerly at construction time and crashed on that null Thruster;
         it now defers/retries the same binding every frame from
         CalcRotorState() until the source becomes available, which is
         guaranteed by the time any Calculate() runs. -->
    <controlmap> TAIL </controlmap>
    <ExternalRPM> 0 </ExternalRPM>

</rotor>
